    "knowledge_point": "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem",
    "problem": "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{x}}{{x}^{2}+1}?$   \n\nI. $[-1,5]$\n\nII. $[0,2]$\n\nIII. $[2,5]$",
    "worked_solution": "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because $\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is not defined anywhere in the subinterval $[-1,0).$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The intervals $[0,2]$ and $[2,5]$ are closed, and the function is continuous on each of these intervals Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on each $[0,2]$  and $[2,5].$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
